    "compositionYear": 105,
    "summary": "After her husband Caecina Paetus is condemned for involvement in Scribonianus's revolt, Arria stabs herself first and hands him the dagger with the words \"Paete, non dolet\" (\"Paetus, it does not hurt\"), an exemplary act of resolve that affirms libertas over submission to imperial tyranny.",
    "translationEn": "Gaius Plinius to his friend Nepos, greetings.\n\nI think I have noticed that, among the deeds and sayings of men and women, some are more famous while others are greater. My opinion was confirmed yesterday by a conversation with Fannia. She is the granddaughter of that Arria who was both comfort and example to her husband in his death. She related many things about her grandmother, no less great than that famous deed, but more obscure; things which I think will be as marvelous to you reading them as they were to me hearing them.\n\nCaecina Paetus, her husband, was ill, and her son was ill too, each—as it seemed—mortally. The son died, of outstanding beauty and equal modesty, dear to his parents no less on account of his other qualities than because he was their son. For him she so prepared the funeral, so conducted the funeral rites, that her husband did not know of it; indeed, as often as she entered his room, she pretended the son was alive, and even that he was getting better, and when, very often, he asked how the boy was, she would answer: \"He has rested well, he has taken food willingly.\" Then, when her long-restrained tears began to overcome her and burst forth, she would go out; then she gave herself over to grief; when she had wept her fill, she would return with dry eyes and a composed face, as though she had left her bereavement outside.\n\nGlorious indeed was that other deed of hers: to draw the sword, to pierce her own breast, to draw out the dagger, to hand it to her husband, to add the immortal and almost divine utterance: \"Paetus, it does not hurt.\" But nevertheless, as she was doing this, as she was saying this, glory and eternity lay before her eyes; how much greater is it, then, without the reward of eternity, without the reward of glory, to hide one's tears, to conceal one's grief, and, having lost a son, still to go on playing the part of a mother.\n\nScribonianus had taken up arms in Illyricum against Claudius; Paetus had been on his side, and, once Scribonianus was killed, was being dragged off to Rome. He was about to board a ship; Arria begged the soldiers that she be put aboard together with him. \"Surely,\" she said, \"you will give a man of consular rank a few paltry slaves, from whose hand he may take food, by whom he may be dressed, by whom he may be shod; I alone will supply all of these things.\" She did not obtain this: she hired a small fishing boat, and in this tiny craft followed the huge ship.\n\nThe same woman, before Claudius, said to the wife of Scribonianus, when she was offering to give evidence: \"Shall I listen to you, in whose lap Scribonianus was killed, and yet you live?\" From this it is clear that her resolve to die a most glorious death was not formed on the spur of the moment. Indeed, when her son-in-law Thrasea begged her not to persist in her resolve to die, and among other things had said: \"Do you then wish that your daughter, if I must perish, should die with me?\"—she replied: \"If she has lived with you as long and in as much harmony as I have with Paetus, I do.\" This answer increased her family's anxiety for her; she was watched more closely; she perceived it and said: \"You accomplish nothing: you can bring it about that I die badly, but that I not die, you cannot.\" While she was saying this, she leapt up from her chair and dashed her head with great force against the opposite wall, and fell down. Revived, she said: \"I had told you that I would find some way to death, however hard, if you denied me an easy one.\"\n\nDo these things not seem to you greater than that \"Paetus, it does not hurt,\" to which they led the way? And yet the one is carried about by great fame, the other by none. From this it follows, as I said at the beginning, that some things are more famous, others greater. Farewell.",
    "originalText": "C. Plinius Nepoti suo s.\nAdnotasse videor facta dictaque virorum feminarumque alia clariora esse alia maiora. Confirmata est opinio mea hesterno Fanniae sermone. Neptis haec Arriae illius, quae marito et solacium mortis et exemplum fuit. Multa referebat aviae suae non minora hoc sed obscuriora; quae tibi existimo tam mirabilia legenti fore, quam mihi audienti fuerunt. Aegrotabat Caecina Paetus maritus eius, aegrotabat et filius, uterque mortifere, ut videbatur. Filius decessit eximia pulchritudine pari verecundia, et parentibus non minus ob alia carus quam quod filius erat. Huic illa ita funus paravit, ita duxit exsequias, ut ignoraret maritus; quin immo quotiens cubiculum eius intraret, vivere filium atque etiam commodiorem esse simulabat, ac persaepe interroganti, quid ageret puer, respondebat; 'Bene quievit, libenter cibum sumpsit.' Deinde, cum diu cohibitae lacrimae vincerent prorumperentque, egrediebatur; tunc se dolori dabat; satiata siccis oculis composito vultu redibat, tamquam orbitatem foris reliquisset. Praeclarum quidem illud eiusdem, ferrum stringere, perfodere pectus, extrahere pugionem, porrigere marito, addere vocem immortalem ac paene divinam: 'Paete, non dolet.' Sed tamen ista facienti, ista dicenti, gloria et aeternitas ante oculos erant; quo maius est sine praemio aeternitatis, sine praemio gloriae, abdere lacrimas operire luctum, amissoque filio matrem adhuc agere.\n\nScribonianus arma in Illyrico contra Claudium moverat; fuerat Paetus in partibus, et occiso Scriboniano Romam trahebatur. Erat ascensurus navem; Arria milites orabat, ut simul imponeretur. 'Nempe enim' inquit 'daturi estis consulari viro servolos aliquos, quorum e manu cibum capiat, a quibus vestiatur, a quibus calcietur; omnia sola praestabo.' Non impetravit: conduxit piscatoriam nauculam, ingensque navigium minimo secuta est. Eadem apud Claudium uxori Scriboniani, cum illa profiteretur indicium, 'Ego' inquit 'te audiam, cuius in gremio Scribonianus occisus est, et vivis?' Ex quo manifestum est ei consilium pulcherrimae mortis non subitum fuisse. Quin etiam, cum Thrasea gener eius deprecaretur, ne mori pergeret, interque alia dixisset: 'Vis ergo filiam tuam, si mihi pereundum fuerit, mori mecum?', respondit: 'Si tam diu tantaque concordia vixerit tecum quam ego cum Paeto, volo.' Auxerat hoc responso curam suorum; attentius custodiebatur; sensit et 'Nihil agitis' inquit; 'potestis enim efficere ut male moriar, ut non moriar non potestis.' Dum haec dicit, exsiluit cathedra adversoque parieti caput ingenti impetu impegit et corruit. Focilata 'Dixeram' inquit 'vobis inventuram me quamlibet duram ad mortem viam, si vos facilem negassetis.' Videnturne haec tibi maiora illo 'Paete, non dolet', ad quod per haec perventum est? cum interim illud quidem ingens fama, haec nulla circumfert. Unde colligitur, quod initio dixi, alia esse clariora alia maiora. Vale.",
