    "id": "polyxena",
    "author": "Ovid (reception of Euripides)",
    "ref": "Metamorphoses 13.439–480",
    "title": "The Sacrifice of Polyxena",
    "tradition": "Roman",
    "period": "Composed c. 8 CE",
    "setting": "Legendary Troy, under the authority of the victorious Greek command, in the aftermath of the Trojan War",
    "deathMode": "Sacrificial death",
    "originalLanguage": "Latin",
    "settingYear": -1184,
    "compositionYear": 8,
    "summary": "A captive Trojan princess sacrificed at Achilles' tomb, Polyxena insists on dying free rather than enslaved, her willing death indicting her Greek conquerors' cruelty while affirming the chastity and dignity Ovid's narrative attaches to her. Euripides' Hecuba is the earlier source; Ovid's version is the one within this corpus's chronological window.",
    "translationEn": "The son of Atreus had moored his fleet on the Thracian shore, until the sea should be calm, until the wind should be kinder. [440] Here suddenly Achilles rose up out of the earth, split wide, as large as he used to be when he was alive, and his face bore the same threatening look it had worn at that time when, savage, he attacked unjust Agamemnon with the sword. \"You are leaving, Greeks, forgetful of me,\" he said, [445] \"and the gratitude owed to my courage is buried with me! Do not do this! And so that my tomb may not be without honor, let Polyxena, sacrificed, gratify the shade of Achilles!\" He spoke; and, his comrades yielding to the pitiless shade, she—torn from the bosom of her mother, whom she, almost alone, was still left to comfort, [450] brave and unhappy, a maiden braver than a woman—is led to the mound and made a victim at the grim pyre. She, mindful of who she was, after she was brought to the cruel altars and perceived that the savage rite was being prepared for her, and when she saw Neoptolemus standing there, holding the sword, [455] his eyes fixed upon her face, said: \"Use my noble blood now—there is no need for delay; or else bury the weapon in my throat, or in my breast\"—and she bared throat and breast at once. \"Surely I, Polyxena, would wish to be no one's slave. [460] By no such rite as this will you appease any god! I would only wish that my death could deceive my mother: it is my mother who stands in my way, and lessens for me the joy of dying, though it is not my death, but her own life, that she has cause to lament. Only—so that I may not go to the Stygian shades unfree— [465] stand back, if what I ask is just, and keep your men's hands from touching a virgin's body! More acceptable to him, whoever he is, whom you are preparing to appease with my slaughter, will be free blood. Yet if any of you are moved by these last words from my mouth—it is the daughter of King Priam [470] who asks this of you, not a captive—give my body back to my mother unransomed, and do not let her buy the sorrowful right of burial with gold, but with tears instead! When she still could, she ransomed with gold as well.\" She had spoken, but the people could not hold back the tears that she herself was holding back; even the priest himself, weeping and unwilling, [475] drove the sword home and tore open the breast held out to him. She, her knee giving way, sinking down upon the ground, carried an unafraid expression all the way to her final fate; and even then, as she fell, her care was to cover what ought to be covered, [480] and to keep the grace of chaste modesty.",
    "originalText": "Litore Threicio classem religarat Atrides,\ndum mare pacatum, dum ventus amicior esset: [440]\nhic subito, quantus, cum viveret, esse solebat,\nexit humo late rupta similisque minanti\ntemporis illius vultum referebat Achilles,\nquo ferus iniustum petiit Agamemnona ferro\n'inmemores' que 'mei disceditis,' inquit 'Achivi, [445]\nobrutaque est mecum virtutis gratia nostrae!\nne facite! utque meum non sit sine honore sepulcrum,\nplacet Achilleos mactata Polyxena manes!'\ndixit, et inmiti sociis parentibus umbrae,\nrapta sinu matris, quam iam prope sola fovebat, [450]\nfortis et infelix et plus quam femina virgo\nducitur ad tumulum diroque fit hostia busto.\nquae memor ipsa sui postquam crudelibus aris\nadmota est sensitque sibi fera sacra parari,\nutque Neoptolemum stantem ferrumque tenentem; [455]\nutque suo vidit figentem lumina vultu,\n'utere iamdudum generoso sanguine' dixit\n'nulla mora est; aut tu iugulo vel pectore telum\nconde meo' iugulumque simul pectusque retexit.\n'scilicet haud ulli servire Polyxena vellem. [460]\nhaud per tale sacrum numen placabitis ullum!\nmors tantum vellem matrem mea fallere posset:\nmater obest minuitque necis mihi gaudia, quamvis\nnon mea mors illi, verum sua vita gemenda est.\nvos modo, ne Stygios adeam non libera manes, [465]\nite procul, si iusta peto, tactuque viriles\nvirgineo removete manus! acceptior illi,\nquisquis is est, quem caede mea placare paratis,\nliber erit sanguis. siquos tamen ultima nostri\nverba movent oris (Priami vos filia regis, [470]\nnon captiva rogat), genetrici corpus inemptum\nreddite, neve auro redimat ius triste sepulcri,\nsed lacrimis! tunc, cum poterat, redimebat et auro.'\ndixerat, at populus lacrimas, quas illa tenebat,\nnon tenet; ipse etiam flens invitusque sacerdos [475]\npraebita coniecto rupit praecordia ferro.\nilla super terram defecto poplite labens\npertulit intrepidos ad fata novissima vultus;\ntunc quoque cura fuit partes velare tegendas,\ncum caderet, castique decus servare pudoris. [480]",
