    "summary": "Valerius Maximus frames Porcia's coal-swallowing suicide as an exemplum of conjugal devotion within his catalogue of moral anecdotes, a distinct genre from Plutarch's biography—worth comparing for how the exemplarity tradition specifically packages her death as a model rather than as narrative.",
    "translationEn": "Your most chaste flames too, Porcia, daughter of Marcus Cato, all ages will honor with the admiration they are due. You, when you learned that your husband Brutus had been defeated and killed at Philippi, since no sword was given to you, did not hesitate to swallow burning coals with your mouth, imitating with a woman's spirit your father's manly end. And perhaps yours was the braver deed, in that your father died by a familiar manner of death, while you were consumed by a new kind of death.",
    "originalText": "Tuos quoque castissimos ignes, Porcia M. Catonis filia, cuncta saecula debita admiratione prosequentur. quae, cum apud Philippos uictum et interemptum uirum tuum Brutum cognosses, quia ferrum non dabatur, ardentes ore carbones haurire non dubitasti, muliebri spiritu uirilem patris exitum imitata. sed nescio an hoc fortius, quod ille usitato, tu nouo genere mortis absumpta es.",
