    "summary": "Composed within a few years of her death, Horace's ode calls Cleopatra a 'fatale monstrum' while conceding she died 'non humilis'—no humble or base woman—refusing to be led in Octavian's triumph. The grudging admiration inside the condemnation is a distinct and more immediate reception than Plutarch's later biography.",
    "translationEn": "[… Odes 1.37.1–20 omitted—the call to celebrate, Cleopatra's drunken plots against Rome, and Caesar's pursuit of her fleet …]\n\n—this fatal monster: [21] she who, seeking to die more nobly, neither feared the sword in womanly fashion, nor with her swift fleet sought out hidden shores, but dared to look upon her fallen palace [25] with a calm countenance, and, brave, to handle harsh serpents, so that she might drink in the black poison with her own body, growing fiercer once she had resolved on death: grudging the savage Liburnian galleys [30] the satisfaction of leading her off, a queen no more, in a haughty triumph—she, no humble woman.",
    "originalText": "[… Odes 1.37.1–20 omitted—the call to celebrate, Cleopatra's drunken plots against Rome, and Caesar's pursuit of her fleet …]\n\nfatale monstrum: quae generosius [21]\nperire quaerens nec muliebriter\n           expavit ensem nec latentis\n                       classe cita reparavit oras,\n\nausa et iacentem visere regiam [25]\nvoltu sereno, fortis et asperas\n            tractare serpentes, ut atrum\n                corpore conbiberet venenum,\n\ndeliberata morte ferocior:\nsaevis Liburnis scilicet invidens [30]\n           privata deduci superbo\n             non humilis mulier triumpho.",
